 of positive integers less than 200 that are divisible by 6 or 15, but not both. I've begun listing out the multiples of 6 and 15 to get a clearer picture of the numbers involved.\n\n**Deriving Sums**\n\nI've now computed the sums for multiples of 6 and 15 individually, and also the sum of their common multiples. The next step is to combine these figures to isolate the numbers divisible by one or the other, but not both.\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]},"llm":{"key":"831a19b9-3899-4a8f-afe3-db1a95113a19","name":"Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite","emoji":"🔀","model":"openrouter/google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite","base_model":"openrouter","temp":1,"formData":{"shortname":"Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite","model":"google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite","system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":"text","tools":"","tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":"","max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"settings":{"system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":{"type":"text"},"tools":[],"tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":[],"max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"progress":{"success":100,"error":0}},"uid":"e1e66496-ac8a-40a7-8989-98fa6cc01cb5"},{"text":40,"prompt":37,"fill_history":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":39},"metavars":{"Expected":40,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","reasoning":"We need to find sum of positive integers less than 200 that are divisible by 6 or 15, but not both. So we need numbers divisible by exactly one of 6 or 15. That is (divisible by 6) union (divisible by 15) minus (divisible by both). \"Both\" means divisible by LCM(6,15)=30. So we want sum of multiples of 6 plus sum of multiples of 15 minus twice sum of multiples of 30 (since those are counted in both sums, but we want them excluded entirely, so we subtract twice). But careful: \"not by both\" means we exclude numbers divisible by both. So we want sum of numbers divisible by 6 but not 30, plus sum of numbers divisible by 15 but not 30. That is sum(6) - sum(30) + sum(15) - sum(30) = sum(6)+sum(15)-2*sum(30). So we compute sums of multiples less than 200.\n\nMultiples of 6: 6,12,..., up to <200. Largest multiple: floor(199/6)=33, so 6*33=198. Number of terms: 33. Sum = 6*(1+2+...+33)=6*(33*34/2)=6*561=3366? Actually 33*34/2=561, times 6=3366.\n\nMultiples of 15: 15,30,..., <200. floor(199/15)=13, since 15*13=195. Number of terms:13. Sum=15*(1+...+13)=15*(13*14/2)=15*91=1365.\n\nMultiples of 30: 30,60,..., <200. floor(199/30)=6, since 30*6=180. Number:6. Sum=30*(1+...+6)=30*(6*7/2)=30*21=630.\n\nNow sum = 3366+1365-2*630 = 3366+1365-1260 = 3366+105 = 3471? Actually 3366+1365=4731, minus 1260=3471. So answer 3471.\n\nDouble-check: Could also compute directly: multiples of 6 not 30: 6,12,18,24,36,...? Actually multiples of 6 that are not multiples of 30 are those not divisible by 5? Since 30=6*5, so multiples of 6 that are not multiples of 5. But easier to just compute sum of multiples of 6 (3366) minus sum of multiples of 30 (630) gives 2736. Then multiples of 15 not 30: sum of multiples of 15 (1365) minus sum of multiples of 30 (630) gives 735. Total 2736+735=3471. Yes.\n\nThus final answer is 3471."},"reasoning_state":{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to find sum of positive integers less than 200 that are divisible by 6 or 15, but not both. So we need numbers divisible by exactly one of 6 or 15. That is (divisible by 6) union (divisible by 15) minus (divisible by both). \"Both\" means divisible by LCM(6,15)=30. So we want sum of multiples of 6 plus sum of multiples of 15 minus twice sum of multiples of 30 (since those are counted in both sums, but we want them excluded entirely, so we subtract twice). But careful: \"not by both\" means we exclude numbers divisible by both. So we want sum of numbers divisible by 6 but not 30, plus sum of numbers divisible by 15 but not 30. That is sum(6) - sum(30) + sum(15) - sum(30) = sum(6)+sum(15)-2*sum(30). So we compute sums of multiples less than 200.\n\nMultiples of 6: 6,12,..., up to <200. Largest multiple: floor(199/6)=33, so 6*33=198. Number of terms: 33. Sum = 6*(1+2+...+33)=6*(33*34/2)=6*561=3366? Actually 33*34/2=561, times 6=3366.\n\nMultiples of 15: 15,30,..., <200. floor(199/15)=13, since 15*13=195. Number of terms:13. Sum=15*(1+...+13)=15*(13*14/2)=15*91=1365.\n\nMultiples of 30: 30,60,..., <200. floor(199/30)=6, since 30*6=180. Number:6. Sum=30*(1+...+6)=30*(6*7/2)=30*21=630.\n\nNow sum = 3366+1365-2*630 = 3366+1365-1260 = 3366+105 = 3471? Actually 3366+1365=4731, minus 1260=3471. So answer 3471.\n\nDouble-check: Could also compute directly: multiples of 6 not 30: 6,12,18,24,36,...? Actually multiples of 6 that are not multiples of 30 are those not divisible by 5? Since 30=6*5, so multiples of 6 that are not multiples of 5. But easier to just compute sum of multiples of 6 (3366) minus sum of multiples of 30 (630) gives 2736. Then multiples of 15 not 30: sum of multiples of 15 (1365) minus sum of multiples of 30 (630) gives 735. Total 2736+735=3471. Yes.\n\nThus final answer is 3471.","format":"unknown","index":0}]},"llm":{"key":"234aef5b-0529-4cc3-bf54-2c4b6b76dabc","name":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","emoji":"🔀","model":"openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","base_model":"openrouter","temp":1,"formData":{"shortname":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":"text","tools":"","tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":"","max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"settings":{"system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":{"type":"text"},"tools":[],"tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":[],"max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"progress":{"success":95.83333333333334,"error":0}},"uid":"d31fbd3f-bae1-492b-a819-624620160f77"},{"text":18,"prompt":17,"fill_history":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":19},"metavars":{"Expected":18,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5","reasoning":"Let me denote the original cost that the shop paid as $x$.\n\nThe shop prices the jacket 40% above what it paid for it, so the price is:\n$x + 0.40x = 1.40x$\n\nThen it puts it on sale at 15% off that price, so the sale price is:\n$1.40x - 0.15(1.40x) = 1.40x(1 - 0.15) = 1.40x(0.85) = 1.19x$\n\nWe know the jacket sells for $142.80, so:\n$1.19x = 142.80$\n\nSolving for $x$:\n$x = 142.80 / 1.19$\n$x = 120$\n\nLet me verify:\n- Original cost: $120\n- Marked price (40% above): $120 × 1.40 = $168\n- Sale price (15% off marked price): $168 × 0.85 = $142.80 ✓\n\nSo the answer is 120."},"reasoning_state":{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me denote the original cost that the shop paid as $x$.\n\nThe shop prices the jacket 40% above what it paid for it, so the price is:\n$x + 0.40x = 1.40x$\n\nThen it puts it on sale at 15% off that price, so the sale price is:\n$1.40x - 0.15(1.40x) = 1.40x(1 - 0.15) = 1.40x(0.85) = 1.19x$\n\nWe know the jacket sells for $142.80, so:\n$1.19x = 142.80$\n\nSolving for $x$:\n$x = 142.80 / 1.19$\n$x = 120$\n\nLet me verify:\n- Original cost: $120\n- Marked price (40% above): $120 × 1.40 = $168\n- Sale price (15% off marked price): $168 × 0.85 = $142.80 ✓\n\nSo the answer is 120.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]},"llm":{"key":"0345fefe-6f71-4ebe-bd3c-f10956f23da0","name":"Claude Haiku 4.5","emoji":"🔀","model":"openrouter/anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5","base_model":"openrouter","temp":1,"formData":{"shortname":"Claude Haiku 4.5","model":"anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5","system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":"text","tools":"","tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":"","max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"settings":{"system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":{"type":"text"},"tools":[],"tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":[],"max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"progress":{"success":100,"error":0}},"uid":"a0ba6ab4-74f8-40e4-9bca-8b38a77494f3"},{"text":18,"prompt":17,"fill_history":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":19},"metavars":{"Expected":18,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"GPT-5.4 Mini","reasoning":"**Calculating price and cost**\n\nI need to compute the cost, denoted as x. With a markup of 40%, the price becomes 1.4x. If there's a 15% sale, the effective price turns into 0.85 times 1.4x, which equals 1.19x. Setting that equal to 142.80 gives me a way to find x. So, I calculate: x = 142.80 divided by 1.19. My check confirms that x equals 120, as 1.19 times 120 gives exactly 142.8."},"reasoning_state":{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Calculating price and cost**\n\nI need to compute the cost, denoted as x. With a markup of 40%, the price becomes 1.4x. If there's a 15% sale, the effective price turns into 0.85 times 1.4x, which equals 1.19x. Setting that equal to 142.80 gives me a way to find x. So, I calculate: x = 142.80 divided by 1.19. My check confirms that x equals 120, as 1.19 times 120 gives exactly 142.8.","format":"openai-responses-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"g