31, Nov 30, Dec 31.\n\nFrom Mar 3 to Nov 19 inclusive. Need count days inclusive = difference in days + 1.\n\nLet's compute day-of-year.\n2024 leap year:\nJan 31\nFeb 29\nMar 31\nApr 30\nMay 31\nJun 30\nJul 31\nAug 31\nSep 30\nOct 31\nNov 30\nDec 31\n\nDay of year for Nov 19: sum Jan-Oct = ? Jan31+Feb29=60; +Mar31=91; +Apr30=121; +May31=152; +Jun30=182; +Jul31=213; +Aug31=244; +Sep30=274; +Oct31=305; then +19 = 324.\nDay of year for Mar 3: Jan31+Feb29=60; +3 = 63.\nInclusive days = 324 - 63 + 1 = 262. Need answer number only."},"reasoning_state":{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer. Need compute days inclusive from March 3 2024 to Nov 19 2024 inclusive. Need ensure count both endpoints. Let's calculate.\n\nYear 2024 is leap year (divisible by 4, century? 2024 not century). Days in months:\nJan 31, Feb 29, Mar 31, Apr 30, May 31, Jun 30, Jul 31, Aug 31, Sep 30, Oct 31, Nov 30, Dec 31.\n\nFrom Mar 3 to Nov 19 inclusive. Need count days inclusive = difference in days + 1.\n\nLet's compute day-of-year.\n2024 leap year:\nJan 31\nFeb 29\nMar 31\nApr 30\nMay 31\nJun 30\nJul 31\nAug 31\nSep 30\nOct 31\nNov 30\nDec 31\n\nDay of year for Nov 19: sum Jan-Oct = ? Jan31+Feb29=60; +Mar31=91; +Apr30=121; +May31=152; +Jun30=182; +Jul31=213; +Aug31=244; +Sep30=274; +Oct31=305; then +19 = 324.\nDay of year for Mar 3: Jan31+Feb29=60; +3 = 63.\nInclusive days = 324 - 63 + 1 = 262. Need answer number only.","format":"unknown","index":0}]},"llm":{"key":"234aef5b-0529-4cc3-bf54-2c4b6b76dabc","name":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","emoji":"🔀","model":"openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","base_model":"openrouter","temp":1,"formData":{"shortname":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":"text","tools":"","tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":"","max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"settings":{"system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":{"type":"text"},"tools":[],"tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":[],"max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"progress":{"success":95.83333333333334,"error":0}},"uid":"8102d115-0a80-4851-bad7-fc5fa913c2d3"},{"text":8,"prompt":5,"fill_history":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":7},"metavars":{"Expected":8,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5","reasoning":"Let me work through this step by step.\n\nSam reads:\n- Day 1: 12 pages\n- Day 2: 12 + 3 = 15 pages\n- Day 3: 15 + 3 = 18 pages\n- And so on...\n\nThis is an arithmetic sequence with first term a₁ = 12 and common difference d = 3.\n\nThe number of pages read on day n is: aₙ = 12 + (n-1)×3 = 12 + 3n - 3 = 9 + 3n\n\nThe running total after n days is the sum of an arithmetic sequence:\nSₙ = n/2 × (first term + last term)\nSₙ = n/2 × (12 + (9 + 3n))\nSₙ = n/2 × (21 + 3n)\nSₙ = n(21 + 3n)/2\nSₙ = (21n + 3n²)/2\n\nWe need to find the smallest n where Sₙ ≥ 400\n\n(21n + 3n²)/2 ≥ 400\n21n + 3n² ≥ 800\n3n² + 21n - 800 ≥ 0\nn² + 7n - 800/3 ≥ 0\n\nUsing the quadratic formula:\nn = (-7 ± √(49 + 4×800/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(49 + 3200/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(147/3 + 3200/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(3347/3))/2\n\nLet me calculate 3347/3 = 1115.67...\n√(1115.67) ≈ 33.4\nn = (-7 + 33.4)/2 ≈ 13.2\n\nSo I need to check n = 13 and n = 14. For day 13, the total is 390 pages, which falls short. For day 14, I need to calculate S₁₄ = (21×14 + 3×14²)/2 to see if it reaches 400.\n\nS₁₄ = (294 + 588)/2 = 882/2 = 441 pages\n\nSince 390 < 400 but 441 ≥ 400, day 14 is when the total first reaches at least 400 pages. I can verify this by checking the daily totals: day 1 has 12 pages (cumulative: 12), day 2 adds 15 (cumulative: 27), day 3 adds 18 (cumulative: 45), and continuing this pattern through day 5 which adds 24 pages.\n\nDay 6 adds 27 pages (cumulative: 117), day 7 adds 30 (cumulative: 147), day 8 adds 33 (cumulative: 180), day 9 adds 36 (cumulative: 216), day 10 adds 39 (cumulative: 255), day 11 adds 42 (cumulative: 297), day 12 adds 45 (cumulative: 342), day 13 adds 48 (cumulative: 390), and day 14 adds 51 pages to reach 441 total. The answer is 14."},"reasoning_state":{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me work through this step by step.\n\nSam reads:\n- Day 1: 12 pages\n- Day 2: 12 + 3 = 15 pages\n- Day 3: 15 + 3 = 18 pages\n- And so on...\n\nThis is an arithmetic sequence with first term a₁ = 12 and common difference d = 3.\n\nThe number of pages read on day n is: aₙ = 12 + (n-1)×3 = 12 + 3n - 3 = 9 + 3n\n\nThe running total after n days is the sum of an arithmetic sequence:\nSₙ = n/2 × (first term + last term)\nSₙ = n/2 × (12 + (9 + 3n))\nSₙ = n/2 × (21 + 3n)\nSₙ = n(21 + 3n)/2\nSₙ = (21n + 3n²)/2\n\nWe need to find the smallest n where Sₙ ≥ 400\n\n(21n + 3n²)/2 ≥ 400\n21n + 3n² ≥ 800\n3n² + 21n - 800 ≥ 0\nn² + 7n - 800/3 ≥ 0\n\nUsing the quadratic formula:\nn = (-7 ± √(49 + 4×800/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(49 + 3200/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(147/3 + 3200/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(3347/3))/2\n\nLet me calculate 3347/3 = 1115.67...\n√(1115.67) ≈ 33.4\nn = (-7 + 33.4)/2 ≈ 13.2\n\nSo I need to check n = 13 and n = 14. For day 13, the total is 390 pages, which falls short. For day 14, I need to calculate S₁₄ = (21×14 + 3×14²)/2 to see if it reaches 400.\n\nS₁₄ = (294 + 588)/2 = 882/2 = 441 pages\n\nSince 390 < 400 but 441 ≥ 400, day 14 is when the total first reaches at least 400 pages. I can verify this by checking the daily totals: day 1 has 12 pages (cumulative: 12), day 2 adds 15 (cumulative: 27), day 3 adds 18 (cumulative: 45), and continuing this pattern through day 5 which adds 24 pages.\n\nDay 6 adds 27 pages (cumulative: 117), day 7 adds 30 (cumulative: 147), day 8 adds 33 (cumulative: 180), day 9 adds 36 (cumulative: 216), day 10 adds 39 (cumulative: 255), day 11 adds 42 (cumulative: 297), day 12 adds 45 (cumulative: 342), day 13 adds 48 (cumulative: 390), and day 14 adds 51 pages to reach 441 total. The answer is 14.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]},"llm":{"key":"0345fefe-6f71-4ebe-bd3c-f10956f23da0","name":"Claude Haiku 4.5","emoji":"🔀","model":"openrouter/anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5","base_model":"openrouter","temp":1,"formData":{"shortname":"Claude Haiku 4.5","model":"anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5","system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":"text","tools":"","tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":"","max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"settings":{"system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":{"type":"text"},"tools":[],"tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":[],"max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"progress":{"success":100,"error":0}},"uid":"c3e0dee5-ecb4-4ac3-8e15-810e66c298db"},{"text":8,"prompt":5,"fill_history":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":7},"metavars":{"Expected":8,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"GPT-5.4 Mini","reasoning":"**Calculating cumulative sum**\n\nI'm working on finding the smallest n for the cumulative sum of an arithmetic sequence where a1=12 and d=3, such that S_n is at least 400. \n\nThe formula for S_n gives me n/2 times (3n+21). I set up an inequality and solve for n to find the positive root. \n\nAfter calculating, I find n equals 14, since S_13 is 390 and S_14 is 441. So, the answer is 14."},"reasoning_state":{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Calculating cumulative sum**\n\nI'm working on finding the smallest n for th