},"type":"default","id":"reactflow__edge-table-43d251c4-8bc3-47a8-b9df-fdc00373bd88Question-prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59question"},{"source":"textfields-7333f737-ebf5-4a52-a631-5ddba1988e59","sourceHandle":"output","target":"prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59","targetHandle":"=reasoning_effort","interactionWidth":40,"markerEnd":{"type":"arrow","width":22,"height":22},"type":"default","id":"reactflow__edge-textfields-7333f737-ebf5-4a52-a631-5ddba1988e59output-prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59=reasoning_effort"},{"source":"prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59","sourceHandle":"prompt","target":"evaluator-2bfc5a0e-8b81-4009-aa23-ea09f1da7690","targetHandle":"responseBatch","interactionWidth":40,"markerEnd":{"type":"arrow","width":22,"height":22},"type":"default","id":"reactflow__edge-prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59prompt-evaluator-2bfc5a0e-8b81-4009-aa23-ea09f1da7690responseBatch"},{"source":"evaluator-2bfc5a0e-8b81-4009-aa23-ea09f1da7690","sourceHandle":"output","target":"vis-c434b57b-f313-4042-8376-8b7ef391d02c","targetHandle":"input","interactionWidth":40,"markerEnd":{"type":"arrow","width":22,"height":22},"type":"default","id":"reactflow__edge-evaluator-2bfc5a0e-8b81-4009-aa23-ea09f1da7690output-vis-c434b57b-f313-4042-8376-8b7ef391d02cinput"}],"viewport":{"x":381.5,"y":462.5,"zoom":0.5}},"cache":{"comment-b2b3e929-625e-4292-b583-059565b0f5fd.json":{},"table-43d251c4-8bc3-47a8-b9df-fdc00373bd88.json":{},"textfields-7333f737-ebf5-4a52-a631-5ddba1988e59.json":{},"comment-f1ec2d6f-d206-4b1c-b3a1-278c17fdc033.json":{},"prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59_0.json":{"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nA train leaves at 9:47 AM. It travels 315 km at an average speed of 84 km/h, stops for 23 minutes, then travels another 145 km at 116 km/h. How many minutes after 9:47 AM does it arrive?":[{"prompt":27,"uid":"8c386021-99f7-44dc-9315-5cadcabf46f1","responses":[28],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":29},"metavars":{"Expected":30,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":27,"uid":"f8df2a75-343a-40b1-85b5-b20ca42a4a0c","responses":[30],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":29},"metavars":{"Expected":30,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[100],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me calculate this step by step.\n\nFirst leg:\n- Distance: 315 km\n- Speed: 84 km/h\n- Time = Distance / Speed = 315 / 84 = 3.75 hours = 3 hours 45 minutes\n\nStop:\n- 23 minutes\n\nSecond leg:\n- Distance: 145 km\n- Speed: 116 km/h\n- Time = Distance / Speed = 145 / 116 hours\n\nLet me calculate 145 / 116:\n145 / 116 = 1.25 hours = 1 hour 15 minutes\n\nTotal time:\n- First leg: 3 hours 45 minutes\n- Stop: 23 minutes\n- Second leg: 1 hour 15 minutes\n\nTotal = 3 hours 45 minutes + 23 minutes + 1 hour 15 minutes\n= 4 hours 60 minutes + 1 hour 23 minutes\n= 5 hours 23 minutes\n\nWait, let me recalculate:\n3 hours 45 minutes + 1 hour 15 minutes = 5 hours 0 minutes\n5 hours 0 minutes + 23 minutes = 5 hours 23 minutes\n\nSo the train arrives 5 hours and 23 minutes after 9:47 AM.\n\nThe question asks for minutes after 9:47 AM.\n5 hours 23 minutes = 5 × 60 + 23 = 300 + 23 = 323 minutes\n\nLet me verify my calculations:\n\nFirst leg:\n315 / 84 = ?\n315 ÷ 84 = 3.75\n0.75 × 60 = 45 minutes\nSo 3 hours 45 minutes ✓\n\nSecond leg:\n145 / 116 = ?\n145 ÷ 116 = 1.25\n0.25 × 60 = 15 minutes\nSo 1 hour 15 minutes ✓\n\nConverting everything to minutes: first leg is 225 minutes, the stop adds 23 minutes, and the second leg is 75 minutes. Adding these together gives 323 minutes total.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nMaya puts $2,400 in an account that earns 5% interest compounded once a year. At the end of each year, after the interest is paid, she deposits another $300. How many dollars are in the account after 3 years, rounded to the nearest dollar?":[{"prompt":0,"uid":"cef23044-ca5a-4de5-9887-7c5cd35af4d8","responses":[31],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":3},"metavars":{"Expected":4,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":0,"uid":"89bbf52d-3a7f-46f5-b0ca-ee082ac0da09","responses":[4],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":3},"metavars":{"Expected":4,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[84],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me work through this step-by-step.\n\nInitial deposit: $2,400\nInterest rate: 5% per year (compounded annually)\nAdditional deposit: $300 at the end of each year (after interest is paid)\nTime period: 3 years\n\nYear 0 (starting): $2,400\n\nYear 1:\n- Interest earned: $2,400 × 0.05 = $120\n- After interest: $2,400 + $120 = $2,520\n- After deposit: $2,520 + $300 = $2,820\n\nYear 2:\n- Interest earned: $2,820 × 0.05 = $141\n- After interest: $2,820 + $141 = $2,961\n- After deposit: $2,961 + $300 = $3,261\n\nYear 3:\n- Interest earned: $3,261 × 0.05 = $163.05\n- After interest: $3,261 + $163.05 = $3,424.05\n- After deposit: $3,424.05 + $300 = $3,724.05\n\nRounded to the nearest dollar: $3,724\n\nLet me double-check this calculation:\n\nStart: $2,400\n\nAfter Year 1:\n$2,400 × 1.05 = $2,520\n$2,520 + $300 = $2,820\n\nAfter Year 2:\n$2,820 × 1.05 = $2,961\n$2,961 + $300 = $3,261\n\nAfter Year 3:\n$3,261 × 1.05 = $3,424.05\n$3,424.05 + $300 = $3,724.05\n\nRounded: $3,724\n\nYes, that's correct.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nA rectangular garden measures 23 m by 17 m. A path 1.5 m wide runs all the way around the outside of the garden. What is the area of the path, in square meters?":[{"prompt":24,"uid":"966a8eeb-e07d-49b9-a0d2-4cec79ea2585","responses":[32],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":26},"metavars":{"Expected":25,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":24,"uid":"fe84482f-9267-42e1-b51c-25baeccb9752","responses":[25],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":26},"metavars":{"Expected":25,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[83],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The garden measures 23 m by 17 m.\n\nA path 1.5 m wide runs all the way around the outside of the garden.\n\nI need to find the area of just the path.\n\nThe total area including the path would be:\n- The outer dimensions would be: (23 + 2(1.5)) by (17 + 2(1.5))\n- That's (23 + 3) by (17 + 3) = 26 by 20\n\nArea of garden plus path = 26 × 20 = 520 square meters\n\nArea of just the garden = 23 × 17 = 391 square meters\n\nArea of the path = 520 - 391 = 129 square meters\n\nLet me double-check:\n- Garden dimensions: 23 m × 17 m\n- Path width: 1.5 m on all sides\n- Outer dimensions: (23 + 1.5 + 1.5) × (17 + 1.5 + 1.5) = (23 + 3) × (17 + 3) = 26 × 20\n\nArea with path: 26 × 20 = 520 m²\nArea without path: 23 × 17 = 391 m²\nArea of path: 520 - 391 = 129 m²","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nWhat is the sum of all positive integers less than 200 that are divisible by 6 or by 15, but not by both?":[{"prompt":37,"uid":"cd38c520-a15e-484b-bb9a-e01827da1e89","responses":[41],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":39},"metavars":{"Expected":40,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":37,"uid":"48cfd8c1-65bc-49cb-acc0-3b9f3484bbbb","responses":[40],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":39},"metavars":{"Expected":40,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[114],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I need to find the sum of all positive integers less than 200 that are divisible by 6 or by 15, but not by both.\n\nFirst, let me identify:\n- Numbers divisible by 6\n- Numbers divisible by 15\n- Numbers divisible by both 6 and 15\n\nA number is divisible by both 6 and 15 if and only if it's divisible by lcm(6, 15).\n- 6 = 2 × 3\n- 15 = 3 × 5\n- lcm(6, 15) = 2 × 3 × 5 = 30\n\nSo I need:\n- (Sum of multiples of 6) + (Sum of multiples of 15) - 2×(Sum of multiples of 30)\n\nThe last term is multiplied by 2 because we want to exclude numbers divisible by both, which means we need to subtract them from both sums.\n\n**Multiples of 6 less than 200:**\n6, 12, 18, ..., 198\nThis is 6k where k = 1, 2, ..., 33\n(Since 6 × 33 = 198 < 200 and 6 × 34 = 204 ≥ 200)\nSum = 6(1 + 2 + ... + 33) = 6 × (33 × 34)/2 = 6 × 561 = 3366\n\n**Multiples of 15 less than 200:**\n15, 30, 45, ..., 195\nThis is 15k where k = 1, 2, ..., 13\n(Since 15 × 13 = 195 < 200 and 15 × 14 = 210 ≥ 200)\nSum = 15(1 + 2 + ... + 13) = 15 × (13 × 14)/2 = 15 × 91 = 1365\n**Multiples of 30 less than 200:**\n30, 60, 90, 120, 150, 180 gives me 6 multiples total.\nSum = 30(1 + 2 + ... + 6) = 30 × 21 = 630\n\nNow I can calculate the final answer using the inclusion-exclusion principle: (3366 + 1365) - 2(630) = 4731 - 1260 = 3471.\n\nLet me verify this makes sense. Numbers divisible by 6 or 15 but not both means I need the symmetric difference—elements in one set but not the other. This equals the sum of multiples of 6 plus the sum of multiples of 15, minus twice the sum of multiples of 30 (since those appear in both sets and need to be excluded entirely).\n\nSo my calculation holds: 3366 + 1365 - 2(630) = 3471.\n\nLet me confirm the arithmetic: 3366 + 1365 = 4731, and 4731 - 1260 = 3471. The intermediate sums for multiples of 6, 15, and 30 should also check out.\n\nFor multiples of 6 under 200, the largest is 198 (33 × 6), giving a sum of 6(1 + 2 + ... + 33) = 6 × 561 = 3366. For multiples of 15, the largest is 195 (13 × 15), yielding 15 × 91 = 1365. For multiples of 30, the largest is 180 (6 × 30), so the sum is 30 × (1 + 2 + ... + 6)\n\n= 30 × 21 = 630\n\nCombining these using inclusion-exclusion: 3366 + 1365 - 2(630) = 3471.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nIf 7 workers build 3 sheds in 12 days, how many days would 4 workers take to build 5 sheds, working at the same rate?":[{"prompt":34,"uid":"9cbbdf40-313b-41bd-8228-78c565930b9e","responses":[35],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":36},"metavars":{"Expected":35,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":34,"uid":"41223ebe-b78c-46a3-90ac-de26950ab7dd","responses":[35],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":36},"metavars":{"Expected":35,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[80],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me work through this step by step.\n\nIf 7 workers build 3 sheds in 12 days, I need to find the rate of work.\n\nFirst, let me find the total \"worker-days\" needed to build 3 sheds:\n7 workers × 12 days = 84 worker-days for 3 sheds\n\nSo the rate is: 84 worker-days / 3 sheds = 28 worker-days per shed\n\nNow, for 5 sheds, I need:\n5 sheds × 28 worker-days per shed = 140 worker-days\n\nIf 4 workers are working:\n140 worker-days ÷ 4 workers = 35 days\n\nLet me verify this:\n- 7 workers, 12 days, 3 sheds\n- Rate: (7 × 12) / 3 = 84/3 = 28 worker-days per shed\n- For 5 sheds with 28 worker-days per shed: 5 × 28 = 140 worker-days needed\n- With 4 workers: 140 / 4 = 35 days\n\nYes, that's correct.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nSam reads 12 pages on the first day, and each day after that reads 3 more pages than the day before. On which day does Sam's running total first reach at least 400 pages?":[{"prompt":5,"uid":"ca1761d8-55e8-4b6d-be4f-8cb13cd8dc7e","responses":[49],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":7},"metavars":{"Expected":8,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":5,"uid":"c3e0dee5-ecb4-4ac3-8e15-810e66c298db","responses":[8],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":7},"metavars":{"Expected":8,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[107],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me work through this step by step.\n\nSam reads:\n- Day 1: 12 pages\n- Day 2: 12 + 3 = 15 pages\n- Day 3: 15 + 3 = 18 pages\n- And so on...\n\nThis is an arithmetic sequence with first term a₁ = 12 and common difference d = 3.\n\nThe number of pages read on day n is: aₙ = 12 + (n-1)×3 = 12 + 3n - 3 = 9 + 3n\n\nThe running total after n days is the sum of an arithmetic sequence:\nSₙ = n/2 × (first term + last term)\nSₙ = n/2 × (12 + (9 + 3n))\nSₙ = n/2 × (21 + 3n)\nSₙ = n(21 + 3n)/2\nSₙ = (21n + 3n²)/2\n\nWe need to find the smallest n where Sₙ ≥ 400\n\n(21n + 3n²)/2 ≥ 400\n21n + 3n² ≥ 800\n3n² + 21n - 800 ≥ 0\nn² + 7n - 800/3 ≥ 0\n\nUsing the quadratic formula:\nn = (-7 ± √(49 + 4×800/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(49 + 3200/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(147/3 + 3200/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(3347/3))/2\n\nLet me calculate 3347/3 = 1115.67...\n√(1115.67) ≈ 33.4\nn = (-7 + 33.4)/2 ≈ 13.2\n\nSo I need to check n = 13 and n = 14. For day 13, the total is 390 pages, which falls short. For day 14, I need to calculate S₁₄ = (21×14 + 3×14²)/2 to see if it reaches 400.\n\nS₁₄ = (294 + 588)/2 = 882/2 = 441 pages\n\nSince 390 < 400 but 441 ≥ 400, day 14 is when the total first reaches at least 400 pages. I can verify this by checking the daily totals: day 1 has 12 pages (cumulative: 12), day 2 adds 15 (cumulative: 27), day 3 adds 18 (cumulative: 45), and continuing this pattern through day 5 which adds 24 pages.\n\nDay 6 adds 27 pages (cumulative: 117), day 7 adds 30 (cumulative: 147), day 8 adds 33 (cumulative: 180), day 9 adds 36 (cumulative: 216), day 10 adds 39 (cumulative: 255), day 11 adds 42 (cumulative: 297), day 12 adds 45 (cumulative: 342), day 13 adds 48 (cumulative: 390), and day 14 adds 51 pages to reach 441 total. The answer is 14.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nPipe A fills a tank in 6 hours and pipe B fills it in 10 hours. Pipe C empties a full tank in 15 hours. If the tank starts empty and all three pipes are open, how many hours does it take to fill?":[{"prompt":9,"uid":"083a60b8-4da0-4ee0-8783-69cf003cbbec","responses":[50],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":11},"metavars":{"Expected":12,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":9,"uid":"d7ca51f2-afea-4ecb-9ce3-be22b2c6f43b","responses":[12],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":11},"metavars":{"Expected":12,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[82],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me denote the tank capacity as 1 unit.\n\nPipe A fills the tank in 6 hours, so its rate is 1/6 tank per hour.\nPipe B fills the tank in 10 hours, so its rate is 1/10 tank per hour.\nPipe C empties the tank in 15 hours, so its rate is -1/15 tank per hour (negative because it empties).\n\nWhen all three pipes are open, the combined rate is:\n1/6 + 1/10 - 1/15\n\nLet me find a common denominator. The LCM of 6, 10, and 15 is:\n6 = 2 × 3\n10 = 2 × 5\n15 = 3 × 5\nLCM = 2 × 3 × 5 = 30\n\n1/6 = 5/30\n1/10 = 3/30\n1/15 = 2/30\n\nCombined rate = 5/30 + 3/30 - 2/30 = 6/30 = 1/5 tank per hour\n\nSo it takes 5 hours to fill the tank.\n\nLet me verify:\n- Pipe A in 5 hours: 5 × 1/6 = 5/6 of the tank\n- Pipe B in 5 hours: 5 × 1/10 = 5/10 = 1/2 of the tank\n- Pipe C in 5 hours: -5 × 1/15 = -5/15 = -1/3 of the tank\n\nTotal: 5/6 + 1/2 - 1/3\n\nCommon denominator is 6:\n5/6 + 3/6 - 2/6 = 6/6 = 1 ✓\n\nSo the answer is 5 hours.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nHow many days are there from March 3, 2024 to November 19, 2024, counting both the first and the last day?":[{"prompt":51,"uid":"110d2730-87e4-4d0b-938d-330b6f8bd43e","responses":[52],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":53},"metavars":{"Expected":52,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":51,"uid":"483fc0a5-47ab-42cc-bccf-564e7deca12c","responses":[52],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":53},"metavars":{"Expected":52,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[94],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I need to count the days from March 3, 2024 to November 19, 2024, inclusive (counting both the first and last day).\n\nLet me break this down by month:\n\nMarch 3 to March 31: \n- From March 3 to March 31 inclusive\n- March has 31 days\n- Number of days: 31 - 3 + 1 = 29 days\n\nApril:\n- All of April = 30 days\n\nMay:\n- All of May = 31 days\n\nJune:\n- All of June = 30 days\n\nJuly:\n- All of July = 31 days\n\nAugust:\n- All of August = 31 days\n\nSeptember:\n- All of September = 30 days\n\nOctober:\n- All of October = 31 days\n\nNovember 1 to November 19:\n- From November 1 to November 19 inclusive = 19 days\n\nTotal = 29 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 19\n\nLet me add these up:\n29 + 30 = 59\n59 + 31 = 90\n90 + 30 = 120\n120 + 31 = 151\n151 + 31 = 182\n182 + 30 = 212\n212 + 31 = 243\n243 + 19 = 262\n\nSo the answer is 262 days.\n\nLet me double-check this calculation:\n- March 3 to March 31: 29 days\n- April: 30 days\n- May: 31 days\n- June: 30 days\n- July: 31 days\n- August: 31 days\n- September: 30 days\n- October: 31 days\n- November 1 to November 19: 19 days\n\n29 + 30 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 31 + 30 + 31 + 19 = 262\nThat confirms it—262 days total.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nIn a class, the ratio of students who walk to school to students who take the bus is 5:7. After 6 walkers switch to taking the bus, the ratio becomes 1:2. How many students are in the class?":[{"prompt":13,"uid":"6a92538a-baa8-4be5-b6ba-c0f6e9a30100","responses":[14],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":15},"metavars":{"Expected":16,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":13,"uid":"a027a35c-f6ab-4ac5-a025-88d9bef4e2bf","responses":[16],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":15},"metavars":{"Expected":16,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[76],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me set up the problem.\n\nInitial ratio of walkers to bus takers is 5:7.\n\nSo let's say:\n- Walkers = 5k\n- Bus takers = 7k\n\nfor some constant k.\n\nAfter 6 walkers switch to taking the bus:\n- Walkers = 5k - 6\n- Bus takers = 7k + 6\n\nThe new ratio is 1:2, so:\n(5k - 6)/(7k + 6) = 1/2\n\nCross multiply:\n2(5k - 6) = 1(7k + 6)\n10k - 12 = 7k + 6\n3k = 18\nk = 6\n\nSo initially:\n- Walkers = 5(6) = 30\n- Bus takers = 7(6) = 42\n\nTotal students = 30 + 42 = 72\n\nLet me verify:\nAfter 6 walkers switch:\n- Walkers = 30 - 6 = 24\n- Bus takers = 42 + 6 = 48\n\nRatio = 24:48 = 1:2 ✓\n\nTotal students = 72","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nWhat is the remainder when 3^100 + 5^50 is divided by 7?":[{"prompt":20,"uid":"6fa738eb-18ea-4ddd-8a41-32a5347fd64c","responses":[55],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":22},"metavars":{"Expected":23,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":20,"uid":"937631c2-e266-46d3-8183-6dbd5b543603","responses":[23],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":22},"metavars":{"Expected":23,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[101],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I need to find the remainder when $3^{100} + 5^{50}$ is divided by 7.\n\nI'll use Fermat's Little Theorem, which states that if $p$ is prime and $\\gcd(a, p) = 1$, then $a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}$.\n\nSince 7 is prime, we have:\n- $3^6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}$\n- $5^6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}$\n\nFor $3^{100} \\pmod{7}$:\n$100 = 6 \\times 16 + 4$\nSo $3^{100} = 3^{6 \\times 16 + 4} = (3^6)^{16} \\cdot 3^4 \\equiv 1^{16} \\cdot 3^4 \\equiv 3^4 \\pmod{7}$\n\nLet me calculate $3^4$:\n$3^1 = 3$\n$3^2 = 9 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}$\n$3^3 = 27 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7}$ (since $27 = 3 \\times 7 + 6$)\n$3^4 = 81 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}$ (since $81 = 11 \\times 7 + 4$)\n\nSo $3^{100} \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}$\n\nFor $5^{50} \\pmod{7}$:\n$50 = 6 \\times 8 + 2$\nSo $5^{50} = 5^{6 \\times 8 + 2} = (5^6)^8 \\cdot 5^2 \\equiv 1^8 \\cdot 5^2 \\equiv 5^2 \\pmod{7}$\n$5^2 = 25 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}$\n\nSo $5^{50} \\equiv 4 \\pmod{7}$\n\nAdding them together: $3^{100} + 5^{50} \\equiv 4 + 4 \\equiv 8 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}$\n\nThe remainder is 1.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nA shop prices a jacket 40% above what it paid for it, then puts it on sale at 15% off that price. The jacket sells for $142.80. How many dollars did the shop pay for it?":[{"prompt":17,"uid":"1f5c87c4-2b3a-4654-98f6-6ca6ac37a8f1","responses":[18],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":19},"metavars":{"Expected":18,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":17,"uid":"a0ba6ab4-74f8-40e4-9bca-8b38a77494f3","responses":[18],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":19},"metavars":{"Expected":18,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[78],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me denote the original cost that the shop paid as $x$.\n\nThe shop prices the jacket 40% above what it paid for it, so the price is:\n$x + 0.40x = 1.40x$\n\nThen it puts it on sale at 15% off that price, so the sale price is:\n$1.40x - 0.15(1.40x) = 1.40x(1 - 0.15) = 1.40x(0.85) = 1.19x$\n\nWe know the jacket sells for $142.80, so:\n$1.19x = 142.80$\n\nSolving for $x$:\n$x = 142.80 / 1.19$\n$x = 120$\n\nLet me verify:\n- Original cost: $120\n- Marked price (40% above): $120 × 1.40 = $168\n- Sale price (15% off marked price): $168 × 0.85 = $142.80 ✓\n\nSo the answer is 120.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nA car gets 34 miles per gallon and gas costs $3.89 per gallon. How many dollars does the gas cost for a round trip where each way is 287 miles? Round to the nearest cent.":[{"prompt":45,"uid":"2cb473ef-4da3-45a2-aa94-22c8e342bdf7","responses":[56],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":47},"metavars":{"Expected":48,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"}},{"prompt":45,"uid":"7fb604d3-3d7a-4482-9a57-1ef060f11d96","responses":[48],"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":47},"metavars":{"Expected":48,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"reasoning":[108],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me work through this step by step.\n\nThe car gets 34 miles per gallon.\nGas costs $3.89 per gallon.\nRound trip where each way is 287 miles.\n\nTotal distance = 287 × 2 = 574 miles\n\nGallons needed = 574 ÷ 34 = 16.882352941... gallons\n\nCost = 16.882352941... × $3.89\n\nLet me calculate this more carefully:\n574 ÷ 34 = 16.882352941176...\n\n16.882352941176... × 3.89 = 65.792352941... \n\nLet me recalculate:\n574 / 34 = 16.88235294117647...\n\n16.88235294117647 × 3.89 = 65.79235294...\n\nLet me be more precise:\n574 / 34 = 287/17 = 16.88235294117647058823529411764705882...\n\n287/17 × 3.89 = 287 × 3.89 / 17 = 1116.43 / 17 = 65.67235294...\n\nLet me recalculate 287 × 3.89:\n287 × 3.89 = 287 × 3 + 287 × 0.89\n= 861 + 255.43\n= 1116.43\n\n1116.43 / 17 = 65.672352...\n\nRounding to the nearest cent: $65.67\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculation of 287 × 0.89:\n287 × 0.89 = 287 × (1 - 0.11) = 287 - 287 × 0.11 = 287 - 31.57 = 255.43\n\nSo 287 × 3.89 = 287 × 4 - 287 × 0.11 = 1148 - 31.57 = 1116.43\n1116.43 / 17 gives me 17 × 65 = 1105, with 11.43 remaining, so 11.43 / 17 = 0.6723529..., making the total 65.6723529... which rounds to $65.67.\n\nLet me verify this using gallons instead. 574 miles / 34 mpg = 16.88235294... gallons. Multiplying by $3.89 per gallon: 16 × $3.89 = $62.24, and 0.88235294 × $3.89 ≈ $3.4323529..., giving $65.6723529... or $65.67 when rounded. I should double-check the division 574 ÷ 34 more carefully—34 × 16 = 544, leaving 30 as the remainder.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]}]},"prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59_1.json":{"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with