oning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite"},"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","signature":"AY89a1/qnw/ZZWylUE8Wjf40/xJnwucbQX9wkKdxIa1qD/ArWV/OlYkPvPbJHB0URQj1H8S77yonjTCsTh+DBj4IohW1fUMNMu2BeqjFoaTu4Go=","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}]},{"prompt":34,"uid":"f25594ce-ec6a-4224-9744-f810edd5aba3","responses":[35],"llm":"Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":36},"metavars":{"Expected":35,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite"},"reasoning":[70],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Calculating New Timeline**\n\nMy focus has shifted to determining the new timeline given the change in workforce and facilities. I'm currently calculating the total worker-days required for the initial scenario to establish a baseline. This will allow me to then project the days needed with the adjusted parameters.\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}]}]},"prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59_3.json":{"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nSam reads 12 pages on the first day, and each day after that reads 3 more pages than the day before. On which day does Sam's running total first reach at least 400 pages?":[{"prompt":5,"uid":"e6387350-d0cd-4345-8e50-7068224d1acc","responses":[6],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":7},"metavars":{"Expected":8,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":5,"uid":"6532a85d-2733-4368-b91e-a89fb120fbdc","responses":[8],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":7},"metavars":{"Expected":8,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[110],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to find the day when the cumulative sum of an arithmetic sequence reaches at least 400. First day: 12 pages. Each subsequent day: 3 more than previous. So pages on day n: a_n = 12 + (n-1)*3 = 3n + 9? Check: n=1: 3*1+9=12, yes. Sum of first n days: S_n = n/2 * (first + last) = n/2 * (12 + (3n+9)) = n/2 * (3n+21) = n*(3n+21)/2 = (3n^2+21n)/2. Set >=400: (3n^2+21n)/2 >=400 => 3n^2+21n >=800 => 3n^2+21n-800>=0. Solve quadratic: n = [-21 ± sqrt(441 + 9600)]/(6) = [-21 ± sqrt(10041)]/6. sqrt(10041) approx? 100^2=10000, 100.2^2=10040.04, so about 100.2. So n ≈ (-21+100.2)/6 = 79.2/6 = 13.2. So n=14? Check n=13: S_13 = (3*169+21*13)/2 = (507+273)/2 = 780/2=390. n=14: (3*196+21*14)/2 = (588+294)/2 = 882/2=441. So day 14 first reaches at least 400. Answer: 14.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nA shop prices a jacket 40% above what it paid for it, then puts it on sale at 15% off that price. The jacket sells for $142.80. How many dollars did the shop pay for it?":[{"prompt":17,"uid":"a139f414-2d0a-46fc-86be-aa806c5aad2a","responses":[18],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":19},"metavars":{"Expected":18,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":17,"uid":"b5f33269-6cc3-4037-9a72-dd99e4b16b32","responses":[18],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":19},"metavars":{"Expected":18,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[85],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to find the original cost. Let cost be C. Price is 40% above: P = C * 1.4. Then sale at 15% off: Sale = P * 0.85 = 142.80. So 1.4 * 0.85 * C = 142.80 => 1.19 C = 142.80 => C = 142.80 / 1.19 = 120. So answer is 120.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nIf 7 workers build 3 sheds in 12 days, how many days would 4 workers take to build 5 sheds, working at the same rate?":[{"prompt":34,"uid":"87edb2f0-743c-4723-b846-cbde5ec68734","responses":[35],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":36},"metavars":{"Expected":35,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":34,"uid":"e332dacb-ff4f-4352-8d38-3f71ca96fb19","responses":[35],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":36},"metavars":{"Expected":35,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[102],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to find the number of days for 4 workers to build 5 sheds. Given: 7 workers build 3 sheds in 12 days. First, find the work rate per worker per day. Total work = 3 sheds. Worker-days = 7 workers * 12 days = 84 worker-days. So rate = 3 sheds / 84 worker-days = 1/28 shed per worker-day. Alternatively, one worker builds 1/28 shed per day. Now, we want 4 workers to build 5 sheds. Let d be days. Then total worker-days = 4d. Sheds built = (1/28) * 4d = (4d)/28 = d/7. Set equal to 5: d/7 = 5 => d = 35. So answer is 35.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nA train leaves at 9:47 AM. It travels 315 km at an average speed of 84 km/h, stops for 23 minutes, then travels another 145 km at 116 km/h. How many minutes after 9:47 AM does it arrive?":[{"prompt":27,"uid":"393fe8a6-6594-4fc9-b6c1-5334f7886c52","responses":[57],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":29},"metavars":{"Expected":30,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":27,"uid":"a1202adc-87d2-451d-b5c0-22576eced646","responses":[30],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":29},"metavars":{"Expected":30,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[106],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to compute total time in minutes from 9:47 AM.\n\nFirst leg: distance 315 km, speed 84 km/h. Time = distance/speed = 315/84 hours. Simplify: 315/84 = (105*3)/(28*3) = 105/28 = 15/4 = 3.75 hours = 3 hours and 0.75*60 = 45 minutes. So 3h45m.\n\nThen stop: 23 minutes.\n\nSecond leg: distance 145 km, speed 116 km/h. Time = 145/116 hours. Simplify: divide numerator and denominator by 29: 145/29=5, 116/29=4, so 5/4 = 1.25 hours = 1 hour and 0.25*60=15 minutes. So 1h15m.\n\nTotal time = 3h45m + 23m + 1h15m = (3h+1h) + (45m+23m+15m) = 4h + 83m = 4h + 1h23m = 5h23m.\n\nSo arrival is 5 hours and 23 minutes after 9:47 AM. That gives 9:47 + 5h = 14:47, then +23m = 15:10. But the question asks \"How many minutes after 9:47 AM does it arrive?\" So total minutes = 5*60 + 23 = 300 + 23 = 323 minutes.\n\nThus answer is 323.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nIn a class, the ratio of students who walk to school to students who take the bus is 5:7. After 6 walkers switch to taking the bus, the ratio becomes 1:2. How many students are in the class?":[{"prompt":13,"uid":"b664e1df-1fd5-477f-beae-52aa9a5db455","responses":[58],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":15},"metavars":{"Expected":16,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":13,"uid":"0591d05a-bfb0-492f-bf34-2b7db4f721d9","responses":[16],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":15},"metavars":{"Expected":16,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[105],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We are given: Initially, ratio of walkers to bus riders is 5:7. After 6 walkers switch to bus, ratio becomes 1:2. Let number of walkers be 5x, bus riders be 7x. Then after change, walkers become 5x-6, bus riders become 7x+6. New ratio = (5x-6)/(7x+6) = 1/2. Cross multiply: 2(5x-6) = 7x+6 => 10x-12 = 7x+6 => 3x = 18 => x=6. Total students = 5x+7x = 12x = 72. Answer: 72.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nA rectangular garden measures 23 m by 17 m. A path 1.5 m wide runs all the way around the outside of the garden. What is the area of the path, in square meters?":[{"prompt":24,"uid":"97d7314b-bbe6-4038-a7be-891f546898c6","responses":[59],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":26},"metavars":{"Expected":25,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":24,"uid":"67e9b896-12a1-4550-8418-05a5b3f8967b","responses":[25],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":26},"metavars":{"Expected":25,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[92],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to find area of path around rectangular garden. Garden dimensions: 23 m by 17 m. Path width 1.5 m around outside. So outer rectangle dimensions: length = 23 + 2*1.5 = 23+3 = 26 m, width = 17 + 2*1.5 = 17+3 = 20 m. Area of outer rectangle = 26*20 = 520 sq m. Area of garden = 23*17 = 391 sq m. Area of path = outer area - garden area = 520 - 391 = 129 sq m. Answer: 129.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nWhat is the remainder when 3^100 + 5^50 is divided by 7?":[{"prompt":20,"uid":"70b774a2-4cb6-4622-8320-1d7eeb78c2e7","responses":[60],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":22},"metavars":{"Expected":23,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":20,"uid":"f1c868b6-7b5d-49b8-9779-5e8e7b7e48fe","responses":[23],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":22},"metavars":{"Expected":23,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[104],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need remainder of 3^100 + 5^50 mod 7. Compute modulo 7.\n\nFirst, 3 mod 7 = 3. Compute powers of 3 mod 7: 3^1=3, 3^2=9≡2, 3^3=2*3=6≡-1, 3^4= -1*3=-3≡4, 3^5=4*3=12≡5, 3^6=5*3=15≡1, so period 6. 100 mod 6 = 100 - 6*16=100-96=4, so 3^100 ≡ 3^4 ≡ 4 mod 7.\n\nNow 5 mod 7 = 5. Powers: 5^1=5, 5^2=25≡4, 5^3=4*5=20≡6, 5^4=6*5=30≡2, 5^5=2*5=10≡3, 5^6=3*5=15≡1, period 6. 50 mod 6 = 50 - 6*8=50-48=2, so 5^50 ≡ 5^2 ≡ 4 mod 7.\n\nSo sum: 4+4=8 ≡ 1 mod 7. Remainder is 1.\n\nFinal answer: 1","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nHow many days are there from March 3, 2024 to November 19, 2024, counting both the first and the last day?":[{"prompt":51,"uid":"5767e0ed-7f67-489d-a391-f6c129d9bc9e","responses":[61],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":53},"metavars":{"Expected":52,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":51,"uid":"8102d115-0a80-4851-bad7-fc5fa913c2d3","responses":[115],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":53},"metavars":{"Expected":52,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[116],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer. Need compute days inclusive from March 3 2024 to Nov 19 2024 inclusive. Need ensure count both endpoints. Let's calculate.\n\nYear 2024 is leap year (divisible by 4, century? 2024 not century). Days in months:\nJan 31, Feb 29, Mar 31, Apr 30, May 31, Jun 30, Jul 31, Aug 31, Sep 30, Oct 31, Nov 30, Dec 31.\n\nFrom Mar 3 to Nov 19 inclusive. Need count days inclusive = difference in days + 1.\n\nLet's compute day-of-year.\n2024 leap year:\nJan 31\nFeb 29\nMar 31\nApr 30\nMay 31\nJun 30\nJul 31\nAug 31\nSep 30\nOct 31\nNov 30\nDec 31\n\nDay of year for Nov 19: sum Jan-Oct = ? Jan31+Feb29=60; +Mar31=91; +Apr30=121; +May31=152; +Jun30=182; +Jul31=213; +Aug31=244; +Sep30=274; +Oct31=305; then +19 = 324.\nDay of year for Mar 3: Jan31+Feb29=60; +3 = 63.\nInclusive days = 324 - 63 + 1 = 262. Need answer number only.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nA car gets 34 miles per gallon and gas costs $3.89 per gallon. How many dollars does the gas cost for a round trip where each way is 287 miles? Round to the nearest cent.":[{"prompt":45,"uid":"131a2264-43b0-495f-a619-4b3c52fe0b34","responses":[48],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":47},"metavars":{"Expected":48,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":45,"uid":"48131f46-64ab-4562-adff-448d52e0f0d1","responses":[112],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":47},"metavars":{"Expected":48,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[113],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer. Need compute. Car mpg 34, gas $3.89/gal, round trip each way 287 miles => total distance 574 miles. Gallons = 574/34 = 287/17 = 16.88235294117647. Cost = gallons * 3.89 = 16.88235294117647 * 3.89. Calculate: 16.88235294 * 3.89. 16.88235294*4 = 67.52941176; minus *0.11 = 1.85705882; = 65.67235294. Round nearest cent = $65.67. Need final as number only: 65.67.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nMaya puts $2,400 in an account that earns 5% interest compounded once a year. At the end of each year, after the interest is paid, she deposits another $300. How many dollars are in the account after 3 years, rounded to the nearest dollar?":[{"prompt":0,"uid":"50e8e328-70a1-4bce-9de7-1bed31badeaf","responses":[65],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":3},"metavars":{"Expected":4,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":0,"uid":"f066d79d-6d27-419f-8110-936fbbba7a81","responses":[4],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":3},"metavars":{"Expected":4,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[117],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer. Need compute. Let's solve. Initial 2400 at 5% compounded annually. At end of each year after interest paid, deposits another 300. Need amount after 3 years. Need clarify timing: At end of each year, after interest is paid, she deposits another 300. So year 1: start 2400, interest 5%=120, balance 2520, then deposit 300 => 2820. Year2: interest on 2820=141, balance 2961, deposit 300 => 3261. Year3: interest on 3261=163.05, balance 3424.05, deposit another 300? The wording \"At the end of each year, after the interest is paid, she deposits another $300. How many dollars are in account after 3 years\" If after 3 years, do we include the deposit at end of year 3? The phrase after 3 years likely after all transactions including third year's deposit? Let's examine: \"At the end of each year, after the interest is paid, she deposits another $300.\" After 3 years means at the end of year 3, after interest and deposit? Typically \"after 3 years\" includes 3 years of interest and the deposits made at the end of each of those 3 years if deposits made each year. Need compute. Let's compute including end-of-year-3 deposit: Year 1 beginning 2400; end interest 120 -> 2520; deposit 300 -> 2820. Year2 end interest 141 -> 2961; deposit 300 -> 3261. Year3 end interest 163.05 -> 3424.05; deposit 300 -> 3724.05 => $3,724. If not including end of year 3 deposit, just before deposit after interest = 3424.05 -> $3,424. Need decide.\n\nLet's parse: \"Maya puts $2,400 in an account that earns 5% interest compounded once a year. At the end of each year, after the interest is paid, she deposits another $300. How many dollars are in the account after 3 years, rounded to the nearest dollar?\" Usually if deposit at end of each year and asks after 3 years, it's after the third year's deposit? Many textbook problems: \"At the end of each year, immediately after the interest is credited, she deposits $300. How much is in account after 3 years?\" That would include the deposit at end of year 3. But phrase \"after the interest is paid, she deposits another $300\" means at the end of each year including year 3, so after 3 years account includes that deposit. However some might interpret \"after 3 years\" as after 3 full years from initial deposit, at which point the year 3 interest and the year 3 deposit have occurred, yes. So $3724.\n\nLet's ensure compound interest calculation: Compounded once a year, interest rate 5%. Each year multiply by 1.05 then add 300 at end. Formula: After 3 deposits at end years: A = P(1.05)^3 + 300[(1.05)^2 + (1.05)+1]? Wait careful: Initial P earns 3 years, deposit at end year1 earns 2 years, deposit at end year2 earns 1 year, deposit at end year3 earns 0 years. So total = 2400*(1.05)^3 + 300*(1.05)^2 + 300*(1.05) + 300. Compute: 1.05^3 = 1.157625; *2400 = 2778.30? Let's calculate: 1.157625*2400 = 2778.3 exactly? 1.157625*24=27.783, times 100=2778.3 yes. 300*1.1025=330.75; 300*1.05=315; +300 = 945.75. Sum=3724.05 => 3724. If excluding third deposit: 2400*1.157625 + 330.75 +315 = 3424.05 => 3424. The wording \"after 3 years\" plus \"at the end of each year ... deposits another $300\" includes the year 3 deposit. Answer 3724.\n\nNeed final only number.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nPipe A fills a tank in 6 hours and pipe B fills it in 10 hours. Pipe C empties a full tank in 15 hours. If the tank starts empty and all three pipes are open, how many hours does it take to fill?":[{"prompt":9,"uid":"76b8dcae-1787-47ec-af5e-e4466a838579","responses":[55],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":11},"metavars":{"Expected":12,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":9,"uid":"f88e6c19-a574-4788-8f0c-3f055135f801","responses":[12],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":11},"metavars":{"Expected":12,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[109],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We are asked: \"Pipe A fills a tank in 6 hours and pipe B fills it in 10 hours. Pipe C empties a full tank in 15 hours. If the tank starts empty and all three pipes are open, how many hours does it take to fill?\" So we need the time to fill the tank with all three open. The net rate is A + B - C. Rate A = 1/6 tank per hour, B = 1/10, C = 1/15 (emptying). Net rate = 1/6 + 1/10 - 1/15. Find common denominator: LCM of 6,10,15 is 30. So 5/30 + 3/30 - 2/30 = 6/30 = 1/5 tank per hour. So time = 5 hours. The answer is 5.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}],"Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\nWhat is the sum of all positive integers less than 200 that are divisible by 6 or by 15, but not by both?":[{"prompt":37,"uid":"ca9bc283-9f48-4d57-aca2-64a35c305651","responses":[71],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":2,"question":39},"metavars":{"Expected":40,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"}},{"prompt":37,"uid":"d31fbd3f-bae1-492b-a819-624620160f77","responses":[40],"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":39},"metavars":{"Expected":40,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"reasoning":[111],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to find sum of positive integers less than 200 that are divisible by 6 or 15, but not both. So we need numbers divisible by exactly one of 6 or 15. That is (divisible by 6) union (divisible by 15) minus (divisible by both). \"Both\" means divisible by LCM(6,15)=30. So we want sum of multiples of 6 plus sum of multiples of 15 minus twice sum of multiples of 30 (since those are counted in both sums, but we want them excluded entirely, so we subtract twice). But careful: \"not by both\" means we exclude numbers divisible by both. So we want sum of numbers divisible by 6 but not 30, plus sum of numbers divisible by 15 but not 30. That is sum(6) - sum(30) + sum(15) - sum(30) = sum(6)+sum(15)-2*sum(30). So we compute sums of multiples less than 200.\n\nMultiples of 6: 6,12,..., up to <200. Largest multiple: floor(199/6)=33, so 6*33=198. Number of terms: 33. Sum = 6*(1+2+...+33)=6*(33*34/2)=6*561=3366? Actually 33*34/2=561, times 6=3366.\n\nMultiples of 15: 15,30,..., <200. floor(199/15)=13, since 15*13=195. Number of terms:13. Sum=15*(1+...+13)=15*(13*14/2)=15*91=1365.\n\nMultiples of 30: 30,60,..., <200. floor(199/30)=6, since 30*6=180. Number:6. Sum=30*(1+...+6)=30*(6*7/2)=30*21=630.\n\nNow sum = 3366+1365-2*630 = 3366+1365-1260 = 3366+105 = 3471? Actually 3366+1365=4731, minus 1260=3471. So answer 3471.\n\nDouble-check: Could also compute directly: multiples of 6 not 30: 6,12,18,24,36,...? Actually multiples of 6 that are not multiples of 30 are those not divisible by 5? Since 30=6*5, so multiples of 6 that are not multiples of 5. But easier to just compute sum of multiples of 6 (3366) minus sum of multiples of 30 (630) gives 2736. Then multiples of 15 not 30: sum of multiples of 15 (1365) minus sum of multiples of 30 (630) gives 735. Total 2736+735=3471. Yes.\n\nThus final answer is 3471.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]}]},"prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59.json":{"cache_files":{"prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59_0.json":{"key":"0345fefe-6f71-4ebe-bd3c-f10956f23da0","name":"Claude Haiku 4.5","emoji":"🔀","model":"openrouter/anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5","base_model":"openrouter","temp":1,"formData":{"shortname":"Claude Haiku 4.5","model":"anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5","system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":"text","tools":"","tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":"","max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"settings":{"system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":{"type":"text"},"tools":[],"tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":[],"max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"progress":{"success":0,"error":0}},"prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59_1.json":{"key":"ddc253f3-0e4c-4151-80a0-adc1a337810c","name":"GPT-5.4 Mini","emoji":"🔀","model":"openrouter/openai/gpt-5.4-mini","base_model":"openrouter","temp":1,"formData":{"shortname":"GPT-5.4 Mini","model":"openai/gpt-5.4-mini","system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":"text","tools":"","tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":"","max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"settings":{"system_msg":"You are a helpful assistant.","temperature":1,"reasoning_effort":"off","reasoning_max_tokens":null,"response_format":{"type":"text"},"tools":[],"tool_choice":"","top_p":1,"stop":[],"max_tokens":null,"seed":null,"presence_penalty":0,"frequency_penalty":0},"progress":{"success":0,"error":0}},"prompt-2804edae-868a-4288-950d-cda1e770dc59_2.json":{"key":"831a19b9-3899-4a8f-afe3-db1a95113a19","name":"Gemini 3.1 Flash-