:"8102d115-0a80-4851-bad7-fc5fa913c2d3","reasoning":[116],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer. Need compute days inclusive from March 3 2024 to Nov 19 2024 inclusive. Need ensure count both endpoints. Let's calculate.\n\nYear 2024 is leap year (divisible by 4, century? 2024 not century). Days in months:\nJan 31, Feb 29, Mar 31, Apr 30, May 31, Jun 30, Jul 31, Aug 31, Sep 30, Oct 31, Nov 30, Dec 31.\n\nFrom Mar 3 to Nov 19 inclusive. Need count days inclusive = difference in days + 1.\n\nLet's compute day-of-year.\n2024 leap year:\nJan 31\nFeb 29\nMar 31\nApr 30\nMay 31\nJun 30\nJul 31\nAug 31\nSep 30\nOct 31\nNov 30\nDec 31\n\nDay of year for Nov 19: sum Jan-Oct = ? Jan31+Feb29=60; +Mar31=91; +Apr30=121; +May31=152; +Jun30=182; +Jul31=213; +Aug31=244; +Sep30=274; +Oct31=305; then +19 = 324.\nDay of year for Mar 3: Jan31+Feb29=60; +3 = 63.\nInclusive days = 324 - 63 + 1 = 262. Need answer number only.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]},{"vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":7},"metavars":{"Expected":8,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","prompt":5,"responses":[8],"uid":"c3e0dee5-ecb4-4ac3-8e15-810e66c298db","reasoning":[107],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me work through this step by step.\n\nSam reads:\n- Day 1: 12 pages\n- Day 2: 12 + 3 = 15 pages\n- Day 3: 15 + 3 = 18 pages\n- And so on...\n\nThis is an arithmetic sequence with first term a₁ = 12 and common difference d = 3.\n\nThe number of pages read on day n is: aₙ = 12 + (n-1)×3 = 12 + 3n - 3 = 9 + 3n\n\nThe running total after n days is the sum of an arithmetic sequence:\nSₙ = n/2 × (first term + last term)\nSₙ = n/2 × (12 + (9 + 3n))\nSₙ = n/2 × (21 + 3n)\nSₙ = n(21 + 3n)/2\nSₙ = (21n + 3n²)/2\n\nWe need to find the smallest n where Sₙ ≥ 400\n\n(21n + 3n²)/2 ≥ 400\n21n + 3n² ≥ 800\n3n² + 21n - 800 ≥ 0\nn² + 7n - 800/3 ≥ 0\n\nUsing the quadratic formula:\nn = (-7 ± √(49 + 4×800/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(49 + 3200/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(147/3 + 3200/3))/2\nn = (-7 ± √(3347/3))/2\n\nLet me calculate 3347/3 = 1115.67...\n√(1115.67) ≈ 33.4\nn = (-7 + 33.4)/2 ≈ 13.2\n\nSo I need to check n = 13 and n = 14. For day 13, the total is 390 pages, which falls short. For day 14, I need to calculate S₁₄ = (21×14 + 3×14²)/2 to see if it reaches 400.\n\nS₁₄ = (294 + 588)/2 = 882/2 = 441 pages\n\nSince 390 < 400 but 441 ≥ 400, day 14 is when the total first reaches at least 400 pages. I can verify this by checking the daily totals: day 1 has 12 pages (cumulative: 12), day 2 adds 15 (cumulative: 27), day 3 adds 18 (cumulative: 45), and continuing this pattern through day 5 which adds 24 pages.\n\nDay 6 adds 27 pages (cumulative: 117), day 7 adds 30 (cumulative: 147), day 8 adds 33 (cumulative: 180), day 9 adds 36 (cumulative: 216), day 10 adds 39 (cumulative: 255), day 11 adds 42 (cumulative: 297), day 12 adds 45 (cumulative: 342), day 13 adds 48 (cumulative: 390), and day 14 adds 51 pages to reach 441 total. The answer is 14.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]},{"vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":7},"metavars":{"Expected":8,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"GPT-5.4 Mini"},"llm":"GPT-5.4 Mini","prompt":5,"responses":[8],"uid":"956e92a6-81ed-431d-bd23-3f0b528dfb64","reasoning":[91],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Calculating cumulative sum**\n\nI'm working on finding the smallest n for the cumulative sum of an arithmetic sequence where a1=12 and d=3, such that S_n is at least 400. \n\nThe formula for S_n gives me n/2 times (3n+21). I set up an inequality and solve for n to find the positive root. \n\nAfter calculating, I find n equals 14, since S_13 is 390 and S_14 is 441. So, the answer is 14.","format":"openai-responses-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"gAAAAABqq1SBhbTT2JZw8qurb7y9ZeBRuhrLJrLpsPzubnVZulfN6_FkgKWQBX3j_OkzL5shESvAEphQD1YcD69C9DY2ll6Eo8q-jYL8Cvz2ZSifmuVrhfIwz6qC1Xv-moz8MVbGKh8aQYZgBCO9auZhQm43ba6m2Cmh6HDQRWlxiD5WEB5UCZVxckDv0JIqYnaLuC4j2XLTuvAShdOv3O6qrtiHcZ1KpEMwSwA_N3zND_n-liDvl1H0_M3pCgwVEXj0oj76dKUmD4nZqYS8aTIEmeI-JeHyG8jAWHFtzHGb9wVjXeNpKUyeh5boNwNkOuFc0LOJdApYyQbmcooWJvOYtz1aP4piAe5VvUgCZkHSYFIAVr7n6dCvaZo6ErvOYLHfjRyK5U003SF2D92j4IM6q41n_DZ0ZvHipqdfDHbcZvnERmmZHxlw1W3M6PDE9PPFaD3IwCH5M4GxIEsAZMFXRKgTPnPQpn4JNQWyE3_lC-3nVtJpcN8_bMkxYIj2iuWb5tKPVrua_suedUfWBRcKbQHCO5ktRwDK7aeD9oM2mXWh8-KozOJGhDtkGP580RLFUsziI8ZSYRRkvYyMM-1q7qe2Gfb_48ZnahdpWy0fmc7avp_PEvU6ca7-BSphXfSuVQMDeFjtnm10XwjKJpWwTia38ualXLv5YrrUyd0eEqh7yDLVUm_jyLA_J4_Qn5MxhYGk8ePgHxEfZOFVpphybKTLtg5pCfsDqdc6JsnKyKyvkO_x3grBfIE9IQzV3vi0Tfi9AB3XM4ikiPQoRiSLmMTo6vc4NODR4HFdbPqOleP1a8sJlsbF6VXLUeSsmW5elYltkkjTuxV2bbDDBLLf6hzQCf6SQdJJmOi-iMf3cuM4d4wAx0D0shhCJPyWBDRCT4rV3m9V-ZpIH2-8P5GpJix5WRSZqGF0yS6AKvTUPCJrn-OKftXqrTcVklZA8PmNEsHaOuUZu0JWn0jPZnATm64lllgT6JHZAu8i6zu0JQPalXBczi7GtqsmfUcc6CCVDUqwDV6Qjb0d_VBEDlpe5rLMA-6KyGB6nc5VBbD5GWgDgilQdXP-Fu7inr4TC4CssCrYuk7XTcw_p0A5jcY7pwGE8Y_9dmAOz1xAv_4XuMM09L5PyHCv7jS6T7A_E2e8rxidKVubVeoFbUtQP8ETdJe3pSwGq_abe6ZouBRleOIVtjBCcfEMGicNxqPyWPNXJg2NrizB6LGEg2ApLEEgUPeeiyKO5G098gnsk6uBw8eQZrjdoXwihMPzyPxnjQRQg217dtgzJjfUCD5SgLglux06nLEPYF4E_4wnGh-ZWRhMPDzDv6y4PcO3HT7GDelhbufqXhsH1qrCwm6Xz2cXKyyIQQojNiia5cf7-JGODTD89K1fr2rP2aIuPwM6ylMI25LEKCbHjqez4ibgz5CVqgce4R6LNJy16wfynwI8S7OMLMe1VaqSqxAztre1GTLq1FEWZySlkI1WQd51kxG5jK6K7b-3cfwNCC4zhABLfjixDgpnxMbL6BoDcR9PfVwY3wg75CUDxO4Hc23kbHIBEX1H3JPiZj9Rhv6g23fpnIkstwoIXxQ090ww-b3RuwCWfI6wGiLiJsSKSuzoXIL-Ecb0fj9JOA_Dk37rdny2kEjpeNaf0i4ff-nrOtEA3b4RDFmQmUXEJs7UkWJNHDVQHiteDj5b4jCMohKa5HYfGZW7hcyKBZKMKem1TmyvUQBJDao51hQsg6-DtLPhugiaAqcwrfLodhGiUAT4H6--I5kwmJk4nbPphp7rfWZ4et7Rla5vDX6g1h7cD1J-onqxWeBgNJVaGQe65EOUuWcBX3rE8hX2og9GMzgUBJnfTduFD8TdT3-DoBuIg0alYZ-rTFi1sidrESai2C_VBT3wGxpDB9H8EkCoqDUzuSg81qWO-l8WPBxOc8MjrN8Zv1KlawGyRGo8cQ==.eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoib3BlbmFpL2dwdC01LjQtbWluaS0yMDI2MDMxN3xvcGVuYWkifQ","format":"openai-responses-v1","id":"rs_07f54f62f1f01620016aab547f62fc87d1aef0848e070fc081","index":1}]}]},{"vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":7},"metavars":{"Expected":8,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite"},"llm":"Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite","prompt":5,"responses":[8],"uid":"97f54caa-71ca-40d6-b41b-3e0b137a1f5c","reasoning":[79],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"**Developing Arithmetic Progression**\n\nI've established the first term as 12 and the common difference as 3. The formula for the number of pages on day 'n' is now clear ($3n + 9$), and I'm working on the formula for the running total after 'n' days using the arithmetic series sum.\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0}]}]},{"vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":7},"metavars":{"Expected":8,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"DeepSeek V4 Flash"},"llm":"DeepSeek V4 Flash","prompt":5,"responses":[8],"uid":"6532a85d-2733-4368-b91e-a89fb120fbdc","reasoning":[110],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to find the day when the cumulative sum of an arithmetic sequence reaches at least 400. First day: 12 pages. Each subsequent day: 3 more than previous. So pages on day n: a_n = 12 + (n-1)*3 = 3n + 9? Check: n=1: 3*1+9=12, yes. Sum of first n days: S_n = n/2 * (first + last) = n/2 * (12 + (3n+9)) = n/2 * (3n+21) = n*(3n+21)/2 = (3n^2+21n)/2. Set >=400: (3n^2+21n)/2 >=400 => 3n^2+21n >=800 => 3n^2+21n-800>=0. Solve quadratic: n = [-21 ± sqrt(441 + 9600)]/(6) = [-21 ± sqrt(10041)]/6. sqrt(10041) approx? 100^2=10000, 100.2^2=10040.04, so about 100.2. So n ≈ (-21+100.2)/6 = 79.2/6 = 13.2. So n=14? Check n=13: S_13 = (3*169+21*13)/2 = (507+273)/2 = 780/2=390. n=14: (3*196+21*14)/2 = (588+294)/2 = 882/2=441. So day 14 first reaches at least 400. Answer: 14.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}]},{"vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":47},"metavars":{"Expected":48,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"Claude Haiku 4.5"},"llm":"Claude Haiku 4.5","prompt":45,"responses":[48],"uid":"7fb604d3-3d7a-4482-9a57-1ef060f11d96","reasoning":[108],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me work through this step by step.\n\nThe car gets 34 miles per gallon.\nGas costs $3.89 per gallon.\nRound trip where each way is 287 miles.\n\nTotal distance = 287 × 2 = 574 miles\n\nGallons needed = 574 ÷ 34 = 16.882352941... gallons\n\nCost = 16.882352941... × $3.89\n\nLet me calculate this more carefully:\n574 ÷ 34 = 16.882352941176...\n\n16.882352941176... × 3.89 = 65.792352941... \n\nLet me recalculate:\n574 / 34 = 16.88235294117647...\n\n16.88235294117647 × 3.89 = 65.79235294...\n\nLet me be more precise:\n574 / 34 = 287/17 = 16.88235294117647058823529411764705882...\n\n287/17 × 3.89 = 287 × 3.89 / 17 = 1116.43 / 17 = 65.67235294...\n\nLet me recalculate 287 × 3.89:\n287 × 3.89 = 287 × 3 + 287 × 0.89\n= 861 + 255.43\n= 1116.43\n\n1116.43 / 17 = 65.672352...\n\nRounding to the nearest cent: $65.67\n\nWait, let me double-check my calculation of 287 × 0.89:\n287 × 0.89 = 287 × (1 - 0.11) = 287 - 287 × 0.11 = 287 - 31.57 = 255.43\n\nSo 287 × 3.89 = 287 × 4 - 287 × 0.11 = 1148 - 31.57 = 1116.43\n1116.43 / 17 gives me 17 × 65 = 1105, with 11.43 remaining, so 11.43 / 17 = 0.6723529..., making the total 65.6723529... which rounds to $65.67.\n\nLet me verify this using gallons instead. 574 miles / 34 mpg = 16.88235294... gallons. Multiplying by $3.89 per gallon: 16 × $3.89 = $62.24, and 0.88235294 × $3.89 ≈ $3.4323529..., giving $65.6723529... or $65.67 when rounded. I should double-check the division 574 ÷ 34 more carefully—34 × 16 = 544, leaving 30 as the remainder.","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":""}]}]},{"vars":{"=reasoning_effort":66,"question":47},"metavars":{"Expected":48,"__pt":"{=reasoning_effort}Reply with only the final answer as a number, with no words, units or working.\n\n{question}","LLM_0":"GPT-5.4 Mini"},"llm":"GPT-5.4 Mini","prompt":45,"responses":[48],"uid":"3af1adb2-3376-4ba5-93a8-15964cb2e790","reasoning":[89],"reasoning_state":[{"provider":"openrouter","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Calculating travel costs**\n\nI need to compute the total miles, which is 574. For gallons, I’ll divide 574 by 34, giving me around 16.88 gallons. Then, I’ll find the cost by multiplying that by $3.89. Doing the math accurately, I calculate 16.88 multiplied by 3.89. From this, I can round 65.67 to reflect the total cost efficiently. I’m trying to be precise, so let’s make sure everything adds up correctly!","format":"openai-responses-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"gAAAAABqq1SBGw-yAK54I_Oc8wX09pWhyvcyhxMAyb744Gc_dFS72X-5Y4LL5gC-iOYrJsJdIMlgryDfwnQuhROFmMkxU9aOzpv5fBe3bSJO_mD2kINBw4h8Kcp3QUEWO6ZvLT8cSdfR7FO8EWf9_HNrRzmTZkUgKRtcOmzvoUAc744Lcq9hCT0-dZqDolJ9aMlotTPOpJI_-Ke6Q_53pgtkTriR3LhJAGRyOvQ5gwFOrY0f_S-6WSs3dQ-_3Y0yZnF1gQhAaXEU279hAUecHgSDrR1zR-HM2gTryn4kt2ekgI9htNv-cBqMOG2E8eRbKxliqbzjlZyPJNY7eplICgM8lbRNTPzKG2ivQE11piDJ6MzwerqV0sptygl3GH72Y8xaSRCDIZ8Q8jeKhlPXIAhazDeQccLXe-K-RJ1VzbKAq5UK-aAoMd2KiF7sZ_rknOVF7_JIV4CkmKwDVqjofnnVwngwTVND-RQME5xS4jfBv5FOa2P9vnp3f90f6hlolhE4dUR7WWRr3kp9Z9C0WjCZlQlrhqkFFSiMY7PZqkgd768x_vDRBItjalfFq-n2HRiV4iQXSVxWqmJhTpmasGT87etfeJKYLBZLcg2TC7wcJu93FgPE-GjjKoX_47giEPHFsH_h-OdnSlXdExMHxje1pTPId-ogB3OaY8qaFo7FZbDtu_jzf6Nw0C2qJq9Aj5XHdkFEpizfEW_vPCR4eJo4rcXq-Hst1z2FxwbDOVFxBc2p-XrIvhzsY_